{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `b09c33bd`, three points of the C4 client.\n\n1. One option, and no second name. `ClientOptions.drafts` becomes\n   the `DraftPort`, and it stays required.\n   - `DraftRead` was the stand-in of C3. Its comment says that C4\n     owns slots and writes (`scope-client.ts:19`). C4 now owns\n     them, so the client makes the read of present values and the\n     observation of another client's change from the port, inside\n     the client.\n   - Only two test files supply `drafts` today. They move to a\n     memory `DraftPort` with seeded drafts. No page uses the\n     client, so no caller needs the old form. Remove the export of\n     `DraftRead`.\n   - Your second point of the rule stands: the client makes no\n     memory store for a caller, and a missing port is no `state`\n     stop. The type requires the port. A caller without types gets\n     a `TypeError` at construction, before any storage.\n   - An author with two draft options must learn which one a page\n     needs. One option has one answer.\n2. Accepted. An operation validates and owns its inputs before the\n   first wait: the patch, the names of a clear, and the slot. Share\n   the validators through exports of the private module. No export\n   of the package.\n3. Accepted, with one sentence as the rule: a call reports what\n   the store holds.\n   - A write that committed returns its key, also when `close`\n     arrived during the write.\n   - The client publishes no view after `close`. The next client\n     reads the draft from the store.\n   - A stop that follows a commit in the same call names the\n     commit. The how-to table's write column states this case for\n     `state` and for `taken`, as it does for the stops of `9930cf7f`.\n\nI add points 1 and 3 to the sixth amendment of the act environment\nnote.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}